      "note": "Pokorny IEW 55-61 heads the article '1. ar-, themat. (a)rē-, schwere Basis arə-, rē- und i-Basis (ə)rī-, rei- ... \"fügen, passen\"'; Watkins/AHD prints 'ar-. Also arə-. To fit together. [Pok. 1. ar- 55.]' — Nourai's root name, language label, gloss and page reference are copied from AHD and are correct. In current laryngealist notation the root is *h₂er-. Two cross-cutting caveats: (a) most of Nourai's Latin/Greek/Germanic material is lifted verbatim from the 1975 AHD appendix (the gloss 'ôrdô: row of threads in loom' is Watkins' own wording), and where Watkins hedges ('Possibly suffixed variant form (or separate root) *ōr-dh-'; 'Variant or separate root *rē-') Nourai drops the hedge — the *rē-dh- branch that carries Old Persian rādiy, Persian rā and English read is reconstructed by most current handbooks as an independent root *Hreh₁dʰ-, and Pokorny himself calls the link of Latin ōrdō to ar- 'ganz fraglich' (IEW 61). (b) The whole Greek→Arabic→Persian branch (harma → 'araba → ارابه) is rejected by every reference consulted: Lokotsch files araba as Turkish, Wiktionary as Turkic of uncertain ultimate origin, Asbaghi as a Persian word arabicised, and Borhan-e Qate' gives no etymology at all.",
